(15. Bc4 {[%cbm Until recently]has been the main answer in the position with hundreds of games played with it. But after experiencing serious problems after 15.Bc4, another move - 15.Bb5 became very popular (even Garry Kasparov has tried it in his game against Loek van Wely in Wijk aan Zee 1999). Those curious readers wishing to learn more about the variation with 15.Bb5 can be redirected to the large theoretical article by Sergey Tiviakov, published in the New In Chess Yearbook 51. Although 6 months passed since the publication, the number of the games played with 15.Bb5 had doubled, the article still remains very up-to-date with almost all evaluations and assessments remaining unchanged.} 
) 15... Bb7 {[%eval +0.30][%brsp Qd3]}  (15... Bd7 {[%cbm both]} )  (15... Na7 {[%cbm and]can not be recommended, since White has clear advantage in both cases.} 
)  (15... Ne7 {[%cbm But], probably, remains the strongest move in the position with the following main line:}  
