(16. Bc4 {[%cbm but]deserves serious attention.} ) 16... Be6 17. Nxe7+ Qxe7 18. 
Bc6 Ra7 (18... Rac8 {[%cbm both ]} )  (18... Rab8 {[%cbm and]are not satisfactory for Black.} 
) 19. Nd5 Bxd5 20. Bxd5 {. White is slightly better here, but with the correct play Black is able to neutralise White plus.} 
) 16. O-O {[%eval +0.27][%brsp Ne7]}  Ne7 {[%diag 2][%eval +0.25][%brsp Bc4]Black's idea in the positions of a such kind is to try to exchange the knight on d5 and later try to free himself from the weak pawn d6, playing d6-d5. When the position becomes open, Black can exploit the strength of his bishops. That's why White aim here becomes obvious: he must try to keep the blockade on d5 and organise the pressure on the black pawns a5 and d6.}  
17. Nce3 {[%eval +0.20][%brsp Rb8]Not the only good move in the position.}  (17. 
Bc4 {deserves very serious attention. The idea is following: after the exchange of the knights, White is going to exchange the light-squared bishops, playing Bc4-d5. White chances here should be preferred. Here follows several games with it:}  
